Jahshaka patch 0065 — AN ORDER-INDEPENDENT VOXEL MERGE, so a voxelisation
bucket can be a material POOL again.

SOURCE **and** MEDIA. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh`
after pulling this (the source half compiles; the media half is staged by Studio
from the SOURCE tree, so the patch loop must have run in that tree either way).
Files: `Components/Hlms/Pbs/{include,src}/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.{h,cpp}`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer.material.json`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer_cs.{glsl,hlsl,metal}`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer_piece_cs.any`, and ONE NEW media file
`Samples/Media/VCT/VoxelMerge_piece_cs.any`. The voxelisation job gains ONE uav
slot (7 instead of 6) and changes nothing else about its bindings. It shares the two VctVoxelizer
files with patches 0061, 0062 and 0064 and `Voxelizer_piece_cs.any` with 0062 —
different regions, applied after all of them.

WHAT WENT WRONG, AND WHY 0062 IS HALF RETIRED BY THIS.

`VctVoxelizer::build()` issues ONE COMPUTE DISPATCH PER BUCKET PER OCTANT, and
each dispatch is sized by the whole octant however few instances the bucket
holds. Upstream's bucket key therefore names only what a dispatch BINDS: the
compute job's variant, the material POOL's const buffer, the vertex/index
buffers, the texture-pool flag — the material SLOT rides per instance.

The voxelisation merged each dispatch's triangles into the RGBA8/RGB10A2 voxel
textures as a RUNNING MEAN (`mixAverage3/4`): load the mean written so far,
weight it by the count, add this dispatch's, store the new mean back — an 8-bit
round trip PER DISPATCH. An average of the same numbers taken in a different
order is the same value only up to that quantisation, so WHICH INSTANCES LANDED
IN WHICH DISPATCH decided the picture. And the normal's "these two surfaces face
opposite ways" test was made WHILE summing, against the sum so far, so the FIRST
triangle a thread happened to reach decided which way the voxel faced. Together
they were 6/255 on `gi.cascade_determinism`'s room between two attach orders of
one scene.

Patch 0062 bought determinism by putting the material SLOT in the bucket key —
one dispatch per MATERIAL makes the partition a property of the scene instead of
of the attach order. It worked, and it cost: on a scene whose 8,001 primitives
each own a material (which is what the editor gives every primitive) the
outermost cascade's rebuild went **86 -> 325 ms**, 3.8x, because thousands of
whole-volume dispatches at ~28 us each is a quarter of a second of nothing.
Scenes that SHARE materials paid nothing.

THE FIX — ACCUMULATE, THEN RESOLVE, IN ONE DISPATCH.

1. A voxelisation dispatch adds its triangles to PER-VOXEL INTEGER SUMS in a new
   accumulator (`mMergeAccumTex`, PFG_R32_UINT, THIRTEEN texels per voxel
   interleaved in Z: albedo sum rgba, raw normal sum xyz, emissive sum rgb,
   FOLDED normal sum xyz — thirteen and not sixteen because only thirteen carry
   anything), on the fixed-point grid `VoxelMerge_piece_cs.any` defines: 12
   fractional bits, one contribution clamped to [0, 16], so a contribution is at
   most 65,536 and 65,535 of them are the WHOLE uint32 bar one contribution. The
   two unsigned sums are therefore added with SATURATION rather than left to wrap
   (the 16-bit triangle counter that would have capped them is the HLSL
   `!typed_uav_load` branch alone; on Vulkan `voxelAccumVal` is an R32_UINT
   counting freely), and saturating addition is still commutative and associative
   — once the ceiling is reached it stays there. Integer addition is exact and
   associative: the partition stops mattering. Upstream's own `voxelAccumVal`
   stays the triangle counter and the divisor, read and written where upstream
   read and wrote it.

2. It then writes the mean of the TOTAL the accumulator now holds into the three
   voxel textures. Whichever dispatch is last at a voxel therefore leaves the
   complete answer and an earlier one's partial answer is always overwritten by
   it — the value depends on the sums, not on who wrote them — so no separate
   resolve pass is needed. A dispatch also stops visiting voxels it contributed
   nothing to (upstream's condition was `accumTris + origAccumTris > 0`, so a
   dispatch re-rounded every voxel an earlier one had filled), which is most of
   them.

3. The "opposite ways" decision moves into that resolve. Each normal also goes
   into a FOLDED sum, folded into one half-space by the sign of its largest
   component, so ANTIPODAL NORMALS ALWAYS FOLD TOGETHER. Write the folded sum as
   U - F and the raw one as U + F, where U is the sum of the normals the fold
   left alone and F the sum, in their ORIGINAL directions, of the ones it turned
   round: then U = (raw + folded)/2 and F = (raw - folded)/2, both exact.
   THE DECISION IS GEOMETRY AND NOT MEMBERSHIP, which is the whole subtlety.
   "Both groups are non-empty" is the wrong question: the fold's seam runs along
   the six arcs where the largest component changes (x = -y, x = -z, y = -z), and
   a smooth or faceted surface crosses those arcs everywhere — (0.72, 0, -0.69)
   and (0.69, 0, -0.72) are SIX AND A HALF DEGREES apart and land one in each
   group. Membership calls that voxel double-sided and the injection then takes
   abs(NdotL) and lights it FROM BEHIND: the leak class, on every sphere,
   cylinder, character and curved wall, and on flat walls built from several
   instances whose matrices differ in the last bit. So ask upstream's own
   question of the two group MEANS instead — are they more than 120 degrees
   apart, `dot(U, F) < -0.5 |U| |F|`, compared squared. The seam pair reads
   dot > 0 and is ONE surface; a plate thinner than a voxel reads
   dot = -|U||F| and is two. Both sums are exact integers, so the answer is still
   order-independent.
   The voxel's normal is then the RAW mean, U + F, when it is one surface — which
   is exactly what upstream stored when its 120-degree test excluded nothing —
   and the LARGER group's mean when it is two, which is what upstream's exclusion
   left behind. Taking the LARGER and not U is load-bearing: a voxel whose
   normals all lie in the half-space the fold turns round (a wall facing -X) has
   U = 0, and storing that leaves it black — measured at four `gi.leak_room`
   assertions.
   TWO RESIDUALS, both recorded rather than hidden. (a) Two normals about 170
   degrees apart CAN both land in U — their largest components on different axes,
   both positive — and then F is empty, the raw mean cancels and the voxel is
   stored with a near-zero normal instead of upstream's majority cluster. It
   needs the pair to straddle a tie within about a degree, and the answer it
   gives is the dark side, not the leaking one. (b) On a voxel holding THREE
   clusters — which is nearly every voxel of a 0.2 m wall at an outer cascade's
   1.9 m cell, since it holds a front face, a back face and an edge — upstream
   keeps whichever cluster its first triangle seeded and this keeps the larger of
   the fold's two groups, and on an edge voxel those differ by a reflection.
   Neither is more right; only one of them is the same answer twice. Measured on
   gi.field_follows' far red bounce: 0.0210 for the pin, 0.0174 here, against a
   GI-off control of 0.0000 — a tie-break, and that suite's bar is re-anchored
   with the three numbers written into it.

4. `VoxelizerBucket::materialSlotIdx` is therefore RETIRED — patch 0062's source
   half, and only that half. Its media half (the `max(x, x)` typo on the
   double-sided flag) is superseded by the resolve computing the flag rather than
   merging it. The bucket key keeps 0062's pointer-free ordering (variant,
   material POOL, texture-pool flag) because a stable dispatch order is still the
   right thing for a renderer to have and now costs nothing.

AMENDED IN PLACE (fix round 1, the lead's second read F1/F3/F4): the flag was a
membership test and is now the 120-degree one; the accumulator lost the three
channels that never carried anything (sixteen texels to thirteen); the two
unsigned sums saturate. The patch was unpushed, and a tree that already carries
the earlier 0065 resets its ogre-next submodule and re-runs `build-ogre.sh`,
which is the same recipe a SOURCE patch asks for anyway.

AMENDED AGAIN (VOXMERGE-2, 2026-09-18) IN THE HEADER AND THE COMMENTS ONLY — no
line of code or media changes, so a tree that carries 0065 needs nothing: the
justification for the texel layout is replaced by the measurement below, because
the reason it gave (a device loss from the 128-bit clear) was attributed to the
wrong mechanism and patch 0067 named the real one.

R32_UINT AND NOT RGBA32_UINT — THE CAUSE CORRECTED AND THE CHOICE RE-MEASURED
(VOXMERGE-2 / render audit ON-6, 2026-09-18). THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY that a
`ComputeTools::clearUavUint` over a PFG_RGBA32_UINT 3D uav HANGS THE GPU on this
driver — `VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST` with `NVRM: Xid 109 CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT`, 8 runs
of 8 on `samples.cleanstart.Showroom{,_2}`, 0 of 4 with that one call removed —
and the thirteen-scalar interleave was bought against it. THAT ATTRIBUTION WAS
WRONG, and patch 0067 is what corrected it: the hazard was
`VulkanVaoManager` handing a freed device-local block to a new resource ONE
FRAME after it was freed while three frames were in flight, and the accumulator's
whole-volume clear was simply the first and biggest write into recycled memory
("it is that hazard, and it is not the clear", 0067's header, with the bisect
table). Patch 0071 then stopped this accumulator being recycled at all — it is
created with the voxel textures and stays Resident.

RE-MEASURED ON THAT STACK (0001-0078, NVIDIA 595.84, RTX 4080 SUPER, the kernel
log followed for every run and every app pid matched against it), a full
RGBA32_UINT arm built and verified — four 128-bit texels per voxel, one clear,
`gi.cascade_determinism`, `gi.leak_room`, `gi.field_follows`, `gi.cascades`,
`gi.cascade_dirty` green on it and BOTH `--engine-selftest` poses byte-identical
to this arm's, so it was a working implementation and not a no-op:

    arm                       cleanstart.Showroom{,_2} rounds   Xid 109
    0065 as shipped (R32)              16 / 16 green              0
    the RGBA32_UINT arm                16 / 16 green              0

THE 128-BIT CLEAR IS CLEAN AT THIS STACK. So the layout is now a COST question
and nothing else, and the cost was measured on the same 8,026-instance lattice
at Photon High (clocks locked 2100/2550, GPU timestamps, three runs an arm, mean
GPU ms per rebuild per run):

    cascade   0065 as shipped (R32)        the RGBA32_UINT arm
    c0        11.7 / 14.5 / 11.3           14.3 / 15.5 / 12.5
    c1        14.9 / 15.4 / 14.5           18.3 / 15.5 / 15.3
    c2        20.2 / 18.3 / 16.6           20.2 / 19.1 / 19.0
    c3        77.3 / 80.4 / 79.5           85.3 / 80.9 / 75.9

THE FOUR-TEXEL LAYOUT BUYS NOTHING MEASURABLE. The run-to-run spread (a sibling
gate shared the box, and the outermost cascade swings 5 ms run to run on a quiet
one too) is larger than any difference between the arms; the best sample of each
arm is 77.3 against 71.9 on the outermost cascade and identical on the others.
That is not surprising once the arithmetic is written down: the merge touches
only voxels a dispatch actually contributed to, the dispatch is sized by the
whole octant and dominated by the triangle raster, and the CLEAR — the one part
that touches every voxel of the volume — writes MORE bytes in the RGBA32 layout
(16 bytes x 4 planes = 64 per voxel against 4 x 13 = 52), not fewer.

SO THE THIRTEEN-TEXEL LAYOUT STAYS, on its own merits rather than on a
misattributed hang: 52 bytes per voxel against 64 is 13.6 MB against 16.8 at
64^3 and 109 MB against 134 at 128^3, and since patch 0071 that memory is
RESIDENT for the voxeliser's life — at Photon High (two 128^3 cascades and two
64^3) the shipped layout holds 245 MB where the RGBA32 one would hold 301. The
RGBA32 arm is kept as a measured artifact at spikes/engine-small-b/ (the amended
patch, its numbers and its suites) should a future scene make the texel count
bite.

WHAT IT COSTS. The accumulator is 52 bytes per voxel — 13.6 MB at 64^3, 109 MB
at 128^3. It was TRANSIENT here (scheduled back OnStorage the moment `build()`
returned, like `mAccumValVox`); PATCH 0071 MADE IT RESIDENT for the voxeliser's
life, because the per-build create/destroy of an image that size is the second
Xid 109 site — see 0071's header, which amends these very lines. Per touched
voxel a dispatch reads and writes thirteen scalar texels beside the three it
always wrote; VOXMERGE-2 measured a four-texel RGBA32 layout against it and
found no difference (above).

MEASURED (lane VOXMERGE-1, spikes/voxmerge-1/, RTX 4080 SUPER, GPU clocks
locked, the 8,026-instance lattice at Photon High, GPU timestamps, two runs per
arm, mean / max GPU ms per rebuild):

